          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Will a 10,000 Watt Generator Run a House? Start With the Real Budget
          </h2>

          <p>
            The short answer is yes, a 10,000 watt generator will run a typical house through an outage, as long as you accept one rule: only one heavy appliance runs at a time. The longer answer depends on three numbers that are almost never the 10,000 printed on the side, and every plan on this page is built from them.
          </p>

          <p>
            The first is the running rating. Most generators sold as 10,000 watt units are rated for 8,000 to 9,000 running watts on gasoline; 10,000 is the starting or peak figure, available for a few seconds while a motor spins up. A true 10000 running watt generator does exist, but it is usually sold under a 12,000 to 13,000 watt peak name, so read the spec sheet rather than the box. For the plans below we use 9,000 running watts on gasoline and roughly 8,100 on propane, which is where the common dual fuel models land after their published derate.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second number is the surge. A generator can briefly deliver more than its running rating, and that headroom is what starts motors. The useful way to think about it is not peak minus running, but how much spare capacity is left at the instant a compressor or pump kicks on. If the house is already drawing 6,000 watts and a well pump needs 3,500 for two seconds, the generator has to supply 9,500 for that moment. A unit with 9,000 running and 11,000 starting watts manages that comfortably; one already sitting at 8,500 running does not.
          </p>

          <p>
            The third number is the one most buyers never calculate, and it is the subject of the next section: how many watts the connection to the house can actually carry. Get that wrong and a 9,000 watt generator behaves like a 7,200 watt one no matter how carefully you plan.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, plan with continuous load in mind. Running any generator flat out for hours shortens its life and leaves no margin for the next motor start. A sensible target is to keep the sustained load at or below about 80 percent of the running rating, which is roughly 7,200 watts on a 9,000 watt unit, and to treat everything above that as room for surges. Conveniently, that is the same figure as the 30-amp ceiling, so the plans below are written to fit under it where they can.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Numbers Every Plan Uses</p>
