        <div id="cord-ceiling" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The 30-Amp vs 50-Amp Connection Ceiling
          </h2>

          <p>
            When a 10,000 watt generator feeds a house, it does so through one 240-volt outlet, one cord and one inlet. That chain has its own rating, and it is often lower than the generator's. A 30-amp, 240-volt circuit carries at most 30 times 240, or 7,200 watts. A 50-amp circuit carries up to 12,000 watts, which is more than any 10,000 watt portable can produce, so on a 50-amp connection the generator becomes the limit again.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical effect is easy to state. On a 30-amp L14-30 connection your sustained budget is 7,200 watts regardless of whether the generator could make 9,000. On a 50-amp connection your budget is the generator's full running rating. The 1,800 watt difference is almost exactly the running draw of a soft-started three-ton air conditioner, which is why the 50-amp question decides whether Plan 2 below works at all. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-hook-up-a-generator-to-your-house/">hooking up a generator to your house</a> covers the inlet and cord hardware for each, and the <a href="/best-generator-with-50-amp-outlet/">best generator with a 50 amp outlet</a> page covers which machines carry the bigger receptacle.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is a second, quieter ceiling inside every 240-volt connection: the two legs. A 120/240-volt generator delivers its output on two hot legs, each carrying half the total. A 9,000 watt generator gives you about 4,500 watts per leg, or 37.5 amps at 120 volts. A 240-volt load like a well pump or a water heater draws evenly from both legs. A 120-volt load like a refrigerator, a microwave or a space heater draws from only one. If most of your 120-volt circuits happen to land on the same leg in the panel, that leg overloads and trips while the other sits half idle.
          </p>

          <p>
            You cannot see which leg a breaker is on without knowing your panel, but an electrician installing an interlock or transfer switch can tell you in minutes, and a well-chosen transfer switch layout spreads the 120-volt circuits across both legs on purpose. If your generator trips a breaker while the total load is well under its rating, leg imbalance is the most likely cause, and moving one heavy 120-volt load such as the microwave or the freezer to a circuit on the other leg usually fixes it.
          </p>

