                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3,600W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,800W of the generator unused</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">50A, 240V</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">12,000W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6,000W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Full 9,000W available</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">50A on propane</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">12,000W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6,000W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 8,100W available</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            If you have not installed anything yet, this is the cheapest decision on the page. The difference in parts between a 30-amp and a 50-amp inlet installation is modest compared with buying a larger generator later, and a 50-amp connection is the only way to use every watt you paid for.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="well-house" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Plan 1: The Rural House on a 240V Well Pump
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the house the 10,000 watt class exists for: a submersible well pump, a gas or propane furnace, a refrigerator and a chest freezer, a sump pump in the basement, and the usual lights, router and chargers. No central air, or central air you are willing to do without. The pump is the load that dictates everything because it is a 240-volt motor with a big starting surge that cycles on its own whenever the pressure tank drops.
          </p>

          <p>
            Here is the arithmetic for a 1 HP pump. Running loads: well pump about 1,100 watts, refrigerator about 700 watts while its compressor runs, chest freezer about 400, furnace blower about 700, sump pump about 900 when it runs, lights, internet and a television about 500, and a microwave of 1,200 used in short bursts. Add it all and the worst sustained moment, with every motor running and the microwave on, is about 5,500 watts. That fits under the 7,200 watt 30-amp ceiling with room to spare.
          </p>

          <p>
            Now the surge. The worst case is the pump starting while everything else is already running, minus the pump's own running draw. That is 4,400 watts of other load plus a 3,000 to 4,000 watt pump start, or 7,400 to 8,400 watts for a second or two. A 9,000 running watt generator absorbs that without noticing. The only time this plan gets tight is on propane at altitude, where the derates stack, or with a 1.5 or 2 HP pump, whose starting draw can run well past 5,000 watts. Our <a href="/what-size-generator-runs-a-well-pump/">well pump sizing guide</a> shows how to read the pump's control box label for the real figures.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Load</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Running watts</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Starting watts</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1 HP well pump, 240V</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,100</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3,000-4,000</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cycles on its own</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Refrigerator</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">700</td>
