                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,000-1,500</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cycles</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Furnace blower (gas)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">700</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,500-2,300</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Needs clean power for its board</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sump pump, 1/2 HP</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">900</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2,150</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Runs in storms</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Lights, internet, TV</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">500</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">500</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Constant</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Microwave</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,200</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,200</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Short bursts</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Total if everything runs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5,500</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Worst start: 7,400-8,400</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fits on 30A or 50A</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Adding the water heater</h3>

          <p>
            With roughly 1,700 watts of headroom under the 30-amp ceiling, this house cannot also run a 4,500 watt electric water heater at the same moment as everything else. On a 50-amp connection it nearly can: 5,500 plus 4,500 is 10,000 watts, which is still more than a 9,000 watt generator. The answer is a heating window. Turn the water heater breaker on for an hour or two in the morning while you hold off on the microwave and keep laundry off, then switch it off for the day. A 50-gallon tank stays usefully hot for most of a day once charged. The routine is spelled out in our guide on whether a <a href="/can-a-portable-generator-power-an-electric-water-heater/">portable generator can power an electric water heater</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            The verdict for Plan 1 is the clearest on the page. A 10,000 watt generator runs a well house essentially normally, with the water heater on a schedule and the dryer either skipped or run in its own window. This is the situation where the class is worth its weight and fuel burn, and where a smaller unit forces you to babysit every pump start.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="central-air-house" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Plan 2: The Suburban House With Central Air
          </h2>

          <p>
            Swap the well for city water and add a central air conditioner, and the question becomes whether the generator can start a compressor. Running is not the issue. A two-ton system draws roughly 2,000 to 2,500 watts once going and a three-ton system 3,000 to 3,800, plus the indoor air handler blower at 500 to 800. Starting is the issue: a compressor's locked-rotor surge commonly runs 6,000 to 8,000 watts on a two-ton unit and 9,000 to 12,000 on a three-ton unit.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Two-ton, no soft starter</h3>

          <p>
            Base load for this house is the refrigerator, a freezer, lights and internet, and the air handler: about 2,400 watts. The compressor runs at about 2,300, putting sustained load at 4,700 watts, well inside either connection. The problem is the start. With 2,400 watts already on, a 6,000 to 8,000 watt compressor surge asks the generator for 8,400 to 10,400 watts for a fraction of a second. A 9,000 running, 11,000 starting watt unit can usually do the low end and sometimes the high end. Owners report mixed results with this exact setup: it works on a cool evening and fails on a hot afternoon when head pressure in the system is higher. Treat it as possible but unreliable, and start the compressor with nothing else cycling.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Three-ton, no soft starter</h3>

          <p>
            Do not plan on it. A 9,000 to 12,000 watt compressor surge on top of any base load exceeds the peak output of the class. Expect the generator to bog, the compressor to stall and hum, and either the generator breaker or the condenser's overload to trip. Repeated stalled starts are hard on the compressor, so this is not a case to keep trying.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Two- or three-ton, with a soft starter</h3>

          <p>
            A soft starter fitted to the condenser ramps the compressor up instead of hitting it with full voltage, and commonly cuts the starting surge of a three-ton unit to around 3,000 to 5,000 watts. That changes everything. Sustained load for the house becomes 2,400 base plus 3,500 for the compressor, or 5,900 watts. The worst start becomes 2,400 plus a 4,000 watt soft start, or 6,400 watts, and even with the fridge compressor also starting it stays under 8,000. That fits a 9,000 watt generator with margin, and on a 50-amp connection it leaves around 3,000 watts for the microwave, a coffee maker or a short water heater window. Our <a href="/what-size-generator-runs-central-air-conditioning/">central air sizing guide</a> covers tonnage and soft starters in more depth.
          </p>

          <p>
            On a 30-amp connection the same soft-started three-ton plan is tighter: 5,900 watts sustained leaves only 1,300 under the 7,200 watt ceiling. It works for cooling and essentials, but the microwave and coffee maker now have to wait for the compressor to cycle off. If cooling is the reason you are wiring in a 10,000 watt generator, the 50-amp inlet is not optional.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE FOR CENTRAL AIR</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">No soft starter, no three-ton air on 10kW.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A two-ton system may start with nothing else running. A three-ton system needs a soft starter, and with one it fits alongside essentials on a 50-amp connection. Never run the AC alongside the dryer or the water heater.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            A heat pump follows the same rules in cooling mode. In heating mode the compressor is still the manageable part; the electric backup strips are not, and they belong in the next plan.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="all-electric-house" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Plan 3: The All-Electric House
          </h2>

          <p>
            The all-electric house is where a 10,000 watt generator stops running a house and starts running a carefully chosen part of one. Every big appliance is a multi-kilowatt resistive load: an electric water heater around 4,500 watts, an electric dryer 5,000 to 5,600, a range oven 2,000 to 5,000 depending on the element, a single large burner around 2,500, and heat pump backup strips anywhere from 5,000 to 15,000 watts. Resistive loads have no starting surge, which is the good news. They are simply big, and any two of them together plus essentials exceed what the class can carry.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start with the essentials, which in this house are the refrigerator, freezer, lights and internet, the heat pump air handler and perhaps a well pump: call it 2,500 to 3,600 watts sustained. Under a 7,200 watt 30-amp ceiling that leaves 3,600 to 4,700 watts for everything else. Under a 9,000 watt generator on a 50-amp connection it leaves 5,400 to 6,500. Now compare that with the appliance list. The water heater fits on a 50-amp connection, and on 30 amps only with the essentials trimmed to the low end. The dryer fits only on the 50-amp connection, and only with the essentials trimmed. The oven fits on its lower settings. The strips do not fit at all in most homes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Heat is the hard part in winter. A heat pump compressor in heating mode draws about what it does in cooling mode, but when outdoor temperatures drop the thermostat calls for the strips, and a 10 kilowatt strip bank on its own exceeds the generator. In an outage the safe approach is to have the strip heat breakers switched off and to run the heat pump compressor only, ideally soft-started, accepting that the house will be cooler than usual in hard cold. If your heat pump cannot heat the house without strips, a 10,000 watt portable will not keep it warm, and a gas or wood backup heat source is worth more than generator capacity.
          </p>

