                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The way to live with this is a rotation, which the schedule below lays out. The rule of thumb is simple: one appliance with a heating element at a time, chosen deliberately, with its breaker switched off when you are done with it. Leave a water heater and a dryer both available on the generator side and sooner or later someone will start a load of laundry while the tank is recovering, and the generator will trip.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your plan requires two of these loads together, for example cooking dinner on the oven while the dryer runs, the 10,000 watt class is the wrong size. That is a job for a 12,000 watt portable on a 50-amp connection or for a standby unit, and our <a href="/best-12000-watt-generator/">12,000 watt generator guide</a> covers what the extra capacity buys.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="load-shedding" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Load Shedding: The Order Things Go Off
          </h2>

          <p>
            Load shedding is just deciding, in advance, what you switch off when the generator tells you it has had enough. It will tell you: the engine note drops and stays low, lights dim when a motor starts, or the generator's own breaker trips. Deciding the order during the outage, in the dark, is how people end up switching off the sump pump during a storm. Decide it now, write it down, and tape it inside the panel door.
          </p>

          <p>
            The principle is to shed by consequence, not by size. The first things to go are the large loads whose absence is merely inconvenient: the dryer, the oven, a second freezer that is already frozen solid. Next are the loads that store energy and can coast, such as the water heater, which holds heat for hours, and the air conditioner, because a house warms slowly. Last are the loads where an interruption causes damage or danger: the sump pump, the well pump if you have no stored water, the furnace in freezing weather, and any medical equipment.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Shed order</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Load</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Why it goes in this position</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1st</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Electric dryer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Biggest load, entirely deferrable</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">2nd</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Oven and extra burners</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cook on one burner or the microwave</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">3rd</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Electric water heater</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Tank coasts for hours once heated</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">4th</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Central air or heat pump</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">House temperature changes slowly</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">5th</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Second freezer, garage fridge</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stays frozen 24-48 hours shut</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Keep</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Main fridge, furnace blower, lights, internet</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Small loads, high value</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Never shed</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sump pump, well pump, medical equipment</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Interruption causes damage or risk</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Two habits make shedding painless. First, when you turn a big load back on, turn it on alone and wait for the generator to settle before touching anything else; that is the same largest-first discipline our <a href="/how-to-use-a-portable-generator/">guide to using a portable generator</a> recommends at start-up. Second, use the breaker, not the appliance. A dryer switched off at its control panel still wakes up the moment someone presses start; a dryer whose breaker is off cannot.
          </p>

          <p>
            A cheap plug-in watt meter for 120-volt loads and a clamp meter for 240-volt circuits take the guesswork out of all of this. Measure what your fridge, freezer and furnace actually draw on generator power once, and you will know exactly how much room each plan really has instead of trusting the ranges on this page, which are typical figures rather than your house.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you would rather not manage loads by hand, an electrician can fit load management modules that automatically lock out a large appliance, typically the air conditioner or water heater, when generator load gets high. They are common on standby installations and work with some portable setups too. For most portable owners a written list on the panel door does the same job for nothing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="daily-schedule" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. A 24-Hour Outage Schedule on 10,000 Watts
          </h2>

          <p>
            Plans are easier to follow as a timetable. Here is a realistic day for the well house from Plan 1, run on a 9,000 watt generator through a 50-amp connection, with a 4,500 watt electric water heater and an electric dryer in the house. The essentials, meaning well pump, fridge, freezer, furnace blower, sump pump and lights, stay on all day and peak around 5,500 watts with everything cycling at once.
          </p>

          <p>
            Early morning, roughly 6 to 8 a.m.: essentials plus the water heater. That is up to 10,000 watts if every motor ran at once, so hold off on the microwave and coffee maker until the tank has recovered for an hour, or switch the freezer off for that hour, which costs nothing. Then switch the water heater breaker off. Mid-morning to early afternoon: the dryer window. Run one or two loads with nothing but essentials on, then switch it off. Late afternoon and evening: cooking on one burner and the microwave, which together are comfortably within budget. Overnight: essentials only, with the generator lightly loaded.
          </p>

          <p>
            For the central air house the shape is different. Cooling takes the afternoon slot instead of the dryer, the water heater gets an early morning window while the house is still cool, and laundry waits for the grid. For the all-electric house, the morning window goes to hot water, the evening window to cooking, and the dryer competes with the heat pump for the afternoon. Whichever house you have, the pattern is the same: one heavy load per window, essentials always on, and nothing heavy overnight.
          </p>

