                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Essentials + cooking</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">AC + one burner or microwave</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Essentials + oven or burners</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Overnight</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Essentials only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Essentials, AC if it fits</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Essentials only</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Light overnight loads also stretch fuel. A generator at a third of its rating burns far less per hour than one near full load, so concentrating the heavy windows into the daytime and letting the machine idle along on essentials overnight is the cheapest way to get through a long outage. The fuel figures for specific models are on our <a href="/best-10000-watt-generator/">best 10,000 watt generators</a> page.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are comparing classes rather than planning around one you own, run the same exercise on a smaller budget. Our sibling guide on <a href="/what-will-a-5000-watt-generator-run/">what a 5,000 watt generator will run</a> shows how the same well house fares with about half the capacity, which is the clearest way to see what the extra 4,000 watts actually buys.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Planning on the 10,000 number</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The 10,000 on the box is a few seconds of starting surge. The figure you live on is the running rating, typically 8,000 to 9,000 watts on gasoline and roughly a tenth less on propane. Build every plan on the running number for the fuel you will actually burn, and treat the peak only as the room you have to start motors.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the 30-amp ceiling</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 9,000 watt generator feeding the house through an L14-30 cord and inlet can deliver only 7,200 watts. Owners often discover this when a plan that worked on paper trips the generator breaker the first evening. If you want the whole rating available to the panel, the connection has to be 50 amps from outlet to inlet.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving two heating loads available</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If the water heater and dryer are both switched on at the panel, sooner or later both will run together and the generator will trip, usually at the worst moment. Turn heavy resistive loads on at the breaker only during their window and off again afterwards, so the schedule enforces itself even when someone forgets.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trying a three-ton compressor without help</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A three-ton central air unit with no soft starter can demand as much as the entire class produces just to start. Repeated stalled starts strain both the compressor and the generator. Either fit a soft starter before the outage season or plan to stay without air conditioning on generator power.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting heat pump strips</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Heat pump backup strips can draw 5,000 to 15,000 watts and switch on automatically in cold weather. On generator power that can overload the system the moment the thermostat calls for heat. Switch the strip heat breakers off before you transfer to generator, and run the compressor alone.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What can a 10,000 watt generator run?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Budget about 9,000 running watts. That covers a 240-volt well pump, refrigerator, freezer, furnace blower, sump pump, lights and internet together, at around 5,500 watts, plus one large load: a soft-started central air system, a 4,500 watt water heater or a 5,000 watt dryer. It will not run two of those big loads at the same time alongside your essentials.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a 10,000 watt generator run a house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It will run most houses through an outage if you manage the heavy loads one at a time. A house with gas heat and either a well pump or central air with a soft starter is a comfortable fit. An all-electric house with strip heat is not: the strips alone can exceed the generator, so you run the compressor or a backup heat source instead.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What will a 10000 watt generator run on a 30 amp cord?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 30-amp, 240-volt connection carries at most 7,200 watts, so that becomes your budget even if the generator can make 9,000. It is plenty for a well house with essentials, about 5,500 watts, but tight for a soft-started three-ton air conditioner plus essentials, which comes to roughly 5,900 watts sustained and leaves little for anything else.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a 10,000 watt generator run central air?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A two-ton system may start with very little else running, but owners report mixed results, especially on hot afternoons. A three-ton system realistically needs a soft starter, which commonly cuts its starting surge to 3,000 to 5,000 watts. With one fitted and a 50-amp connection, cooling plus essentials fits with about 3,000 watts to spare.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a 10000 running watt generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is a generator rated to sustain 10,000 watts, rather than one that peaks at 10,000. Those models are usually marketed at 12,000 to 13,000 starting watts. If you specifically need 10,000 watts sustained, for example to run central air and a water heater at once, check the running rating on the spec sheet rather than the name on the box.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a 10,000 watt generator run an electric dryer and water heater together?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not alongside your essentials. A dryer at 5,000 to 5,600 watts plus a water heater at 4,500 already reaches 9,500 to 10,100 watts before the fridge or furnace. Run them in separate windows: the water heater for an hour or two in the morning, then the dryer later with its breaker switched off when the load finishes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know which loads to switch off first?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Shed by consequence rather than size. The dryer and oven go first because they are deferrable, then the water heater and air conditioning because the house and tank coast for hours, then a second freezer. Keep the fridge, furnace blower and lights, and never shed the sump pump, a well pump you depend on or medical equipment.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
